{"answers":[{"question_id":"Q004_ferritin_like_functional_divergence","answer":"SCOPe places ferritins, ribonucleotide-reductase-like proteins, and manganese catalases in distinct families within the ferritin-like superfamily (a.25.1), in the ferritin-like fold (a.25) of the all-α class. Their superfamily grouping supports probable common ancestry despite substantial functional divergence; sharing only a fold or class would not establish that evolutionary relationship.

The conserved feature is a predominantly α-helical bundle architecture with related metal-binding arrangements, rather than an identical reaction. Ferritins typically oxidize Fe(II) and sequester iron in a mineral core inside an oligomeric cage. Canonical class I ribonucleotide reductase R2 proteins use a metal-associated radical-generating system to support nucleotide reduction by the catalytic R1 subunit; R2 itself is not the nucleotide-reduction active site. Manganese catalases use a binuclear manganese center to disproportionate hydrogen peroxide into water and oxygen. Metal identity, ligand environment, substrate access, and assembly help enable these different chemistries.

Thus, superfamily membership does not justify transferring iron-storage activity, ribonucleotide-reductase function, catalase activity, metal specificity, or oligomeric state among these families. Even the label 'ribonucleotide-reductase-like' does not guarantee a canonical R2 function. A cross-family structure can inform modeling of the conserved core, but cannot by itself validate an active-site mechanism or functional annotation. Functional assignment requires sequence and ligand conservation, appropriate domain and assembly context, and independent biochemical evidence for metal occupancy and activity. Representatives should span the distinct families when assessing functional diversity; one ferritin-like structure cannot represent all three chemistries.","evidence_used":["pretrained model knowledge"]}]}
